@meta
id: 27-16
title: Teach it a key
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: mapping
par: 80
minutes: 8
vscode: a snippet, a keybindings.json entry, and a restart
open: standup.md

@brief
Ten carry-over items from the sprint. Every one of them has to become a
task line: a `- [ ] ` checkbox in front, and ` (unassigned)` on the end.

    refund window comparison uses local time
    - [ ] refund window comparison uses local time (unassigned)

Two edits per line, at opposite ends of it. `.` will not help you — it
repeats the last change, and there are two.

So make it one change. A mapping binds a key to a sequence of keys; press
the key and vim behaves as if you had typed the sequence. Bind one, then
walk down the file pressing it.

Ten lines is roughly the point where this pays for itself. Fifty lines and
it is not close.

@keys
| `:nnoremap - I- [ ] <Esc>A (unassigned)<Esc>` | bind `-` in normal mode |
| `:nnoremap` | list every normal-mode mapping |
| `:nunmap -` | remove that one |
| `<Esc>` `<CR>` `<Tab>` `<C-a>` | how special keys are spelled inside a mapping |

@hints
The edit is two changes, so `.` cannot repeat it. Make vim treat the pair
as one keystroke.
--
`:nnoremap <key> <the keys you would have typed>`. Pick a normal-mode key
you are not using — `-` is a good throwaway.
--
`:nnoremap - I- [ ] <Esc>A (unassigned)<Esc>` `Enter`, then `3G` and
`-j` down the ten lines.

@solution
    :nnoremap - I- [ ] <Esc>A (unassigned)<Esc><Enter>
    3G  -j-j-j-j-j-j-j-j-j-  :wq<Enter>

Sixty-nine keystrokes. By hand it is closer to two hundred and forty, and
every one of those is a chance to typo a bracket.

Read the command name: `n` `nore` `map`. Normal mode, non-recursive, map.
There is one for each mode — `nnoremap`, `inoremap`, `vnoremap`,
`xnoremap`, `onoremap`, `cnoremap` — and a bare `noremap` that covers
several at once, which is nearly always a mistake because a key that is
convenient in normal mode is usually a letter you need in insert mode.

Inside the right-hand side, special keys are written in angle brackets:
`<Esc>`, `<CR>`, `<Tab>`, `<Space>`, `<C-a>` for Ctrl-A, `<A-x>` for
Alt-X, `<F5>`. You cannot press Escape while typing the command — that
would cancel the command line — so you spell it.

Choosing the key is the other half. Vim has few genuinely free keys, and
the ones people usually take are `-`, `<Space>`, `<CR>` and `<BS>` in
normal mode, the function keys, and anything at all behind a leader, which
is the lesson after next. Before you take a key, find out what you are
giving up: `:help -` will tell you it moves to the first non-blank of the
line above, and you will decide you do not care.

Mappings you make with `:nnoremap` at the command line last until you quit.
A mapping you like goes in your `.vimrc`, where it is the same line without
the colon.

